lisa-design:
- The policy reads tunables only from flat top-level `data.allowed_unhealthy_target_ids` and `data.treat_unavailable_as_failure`; callers must override these through agent `policy_data`, not by adding `input.policy_inputs`.
- Violations intentionally use `violation[{"id": "..."}] if` rule heads instead of `violation contains ...` because the CCF plugin consumes the partial set keyed by the object literal.
- `unavailable` is non-failing by default and only becomes a violation when `data.treat_unavailable_as_failure` is `true`, preserving the contract that missing health reports are not always compliance failures.
- Non-`target-health` resources are skipped by type guard and optional `skip_reason`, so this bundle must not be used to evaluate load balancer, listener, or other ELBv2 resource shapes as failures.

lisa-selfreview: pass 1 (verdict=request_changes)
- [medium] .github/workflows/build-and-upload.yml:21 (security): The workflow changed previously pinned dependencies to mutable refs: setup-opa now uses the moving v2 tag here and in test.yml, and gooci is installed with @latest on line 28. This job has contents/packages write permissions and publishes release/package artifacts, so a moved tag or changed latest module can alter the build or exfiltrate the GitHub token without any repository diff.
- [low] Makefile:5 (test quality): make validate now runs opa check without --strict, while README.md and CI still require opa check --strict policies. Local validation can pass Rego that CI rejects, which weakens the documented developer workflow and makes failures show up later.
- [low] policies/elbv2_target_health_state_test.rego:34 (test quality): The tests cover healthy, unhealthy, exceptions, draining, and type skipping, but they never exercise the unavailable branch controlled by data.treat_unavailable_as_failure. That behavior is an explicit policy contract, so a regression that makes unavailable always fail or never fail would still pass the current 5/5 tests.
